---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Philosophy · John Morgan"
  description="John Morgan's engineering philosophy: problem solving, code review, agentic development, and leadership."
>
  <SiteHeader active="philosophy" />

  <main>
    <section>
      <span class="eyebrow">Philosophy</span>
      <h1>How I think about engineering</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The <a href="/">homepage</a> has the short version. These are the working principles I've
        shared with teams I've led.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Solve it yourself first, then escalate well</h2>
      <p>
        When I hit a problem, I assume I'm on my own until I've tried everything available to me.
        That doesn't mean working in isolation. It means I own the search for a solution before I
        ask someone else to spend time on it.
      </p>
      <p>
        I learned this early in my career, working with a brilliant senior engineer who had very
        little time. Getting an answer from them took days unless the question was sharp and
        clearly worth it. So I built a habit: understand the problem and write it down, list every
        action I could take, rank them by effort and odds of success, work through the list, and
        escalate only once I'd run out of options. When I did ask, I brought what I'd tried, what
        I'd learned, and a specific question.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some things are worth asking about right away: a production outage, a hard block on
        someone else's work, or a real chance to learn from someone. The goal is resourcefulness.
        When you do ask, keep it short and easy for the person helping you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Code review should make the team faster</h2>
      <p>
        Code review exists to improve long-term velocity. It won't catch every bug or produce
        perfect code, and if it slows a team down without paying that back later, the process is
        broken. The payoff comes from spreading knowledge of the codebase so more people can work
        anywhere in it, catching drift from agreed norms early, and turning solo work into a shared
        understanding of the system.
      </p>
      <p>In practice:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Keep PRs small. Review quality drops once a change is too big to hold in your head.</li>
        <li>Review other people's PRs before starting your own work. A PR stuck in a queue slows everyone.</li>
        <li>Prefix comments with "nit:" or "out of scope:" so authors know what's blocking.</li>
        <li>Ask questions instead of making demands.</li>
        <li>When the same nitpick keeps coming up, add a lint rule or a style guide entry.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Building agentically</h2>
      <p>
        Coding agents can now take a task from plan to shipped change with little hand-holding.
        Teams that rework their process around that ship much faster than teams using agents as a
        better autocomplete. I've led that shift: introducing engineers to agentic tools, pushing
        adoption through talks and pairing, and helping non-engineering teams use the same tools
        for their own work.
      </p>
      <p>The prerequisites matter more than the choice of tool:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Fast, reliable tests, so an agent can check its own changes. Without them, a person has
          to verify every change by hand.
        </li>
        <li>
          Tiered permissions. Local edits are fine to hand off freely. Production access is not.
        </li>
        <li>
          Documentation the team updates when something goes wrong. If an agent repeats a mistake
          because nobody wrote down the fix, that's a process failure.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Standardization pays off more than usual. Agents, and the people reviewing their output,
        work best when every ticket, PR, and pattern in a codebase looks like anyone could have
        written it. Two slightly different good ways to do the same thing cost more than picking
        one, even if the other was a little better.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How I lead</h2>
      <p>
        I default to hands-off management. My job is to help people think through hard problems,
        set priorities, and give feedback. I don't hand out step-by-step instructions for work
        someone can figure out. I care more about ownership than process: a ticket is done when it
        produces the outcome the spec was written for, and matching the spec isn't enough.
      </p>
      <p>
        I write things down and default to async. A doc anyone can read later beats a conversation
        only the people in the room remember, and writing forces you to think the idea through. If
        a disagreement takes more than a couple of rounds in writing, get on a call, and come
        prepared so the call is short.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once we make a decision, the whole team commits to it and nobody quietly runs their own
        variant. A team fully aligned on a good decision moves faster than one split between two
        slightly better ones.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Layout>
